      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{5}{2}.$ Since there is a jump at $x=- \\frac{5}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{5}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{3}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=- \\frac{3}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{3}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=- \\frac{1}{2}.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{1}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/2ad32afa5a2d6894f0139981a9c9aaccc00220d91f04589dc96835447c59e63f.png)",
