      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Because there is a jump at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=\\frac{7}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\".",
