  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186068695,
      "id": "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c",
      "title": "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/5af2fe18fe46cc935fd8d03a30c15501d01d5153532986badf27b4daf8f86e48.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Use the unit-spaced tick marks to locate the listed points. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=\\frac{3}{2}$\n- **II.** $x=\\frac{5}{2}$\n- **III.** $x=\\frac{7}{2}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since there is a jump at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/1678627c08b33a053aa21076f733093a5f6a9f8a270bdb2b1df509dd943175e7.png)",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
