    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/e58de32becb37bf386a6314a3449d31a0ba138871cb459754099c4cda3b25c99.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Use the unit-spaced tick marks to locate the listed points. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=\\frac{7}{2}$\n- **II.** $x=\\frac{9}{2}$\n- **III.** $x=\\frac{11}{2}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Since there is a jump at $x=\\frac{7}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=\\frac{9}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{11}{2}.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{11}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/c84ef986c2c4201919ebc98030212b14aaf4da37969ab09b52b03913e63a38e8.png)",
